1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:06,325
You add a reranker to your RAG pipeline.

2
00:00:06,625 --> 00:00:10,325
The demo looks better. nDCG
goes up a point.

3
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:12,375
Did it help?

4
00:00:12,675 --> 00:00:16,050
Or did you just add latency and
a new way to fail?

5
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:21,123
In this video, we'll look at how to add a
reranker, and how to prove it was worth

6
00:00:21,123 --> 00:00:21,300
it.

7
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:24,117
Here's the core idea.

8
00:00:24,417 --> 00:00:31,107
A cross-encoder reranker rescores the first
stage's top 50-200 candidates, and

9
00:00:31,107 --> 00:00:33,767
passes the top 5-20 to the LLM.

10
00:00:34,383 --> 00:00:37,508
Then you judge it with a
controlled comparison.

11
00:00:37,808 --> 00:00:43,058
Offline, on a labeled query set, at the
cutoff the LLM actually sees.

12
00:00:43,358 --> 00:00:49,408
Online, with an A/B test that uses p95
latency and cost as guardrails.

13
00:00:49,708 --> 00:00:55,126
The reranker helped only if the quality gain
survives the latency budget, and holds

14
00:00:55,126 --> 00:00:56,758
on every important slice.

15
00:00:57,783 --> 00:01:00,883
Before adding anything, pin
down four things.

16
00:01:01,183 --> 00:01:05,008
First, what's the first stage's
recall at the candidate depth?

17
00:01:05,308 --> 00:01:08,533
A reranker can only reorder
what it's given.

18
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:16,058
If recall at 100 is 0.70, the ceiling is
0.70, and the fix belongs upstream.

19
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:19,633
Second, how much latency budget is left?

20
00:01:19,933 --> 00:01:26,133
A typical reranking slot is
50-250 ms at p95.

21
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:29,933
That bounds the model size, and
the candidate depth.

22
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:33,100
Third, hosted or self-hosted?

23
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,275
Hosted APIs like Cohere Rerank
are fast to adopt.

24
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:40,300
Self-hosted models give you control.

25
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:43,650
Data residency may decide this for you.

26
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:49,400
And fourth, how many passages does
the LLM consume, 5 or 20?

27
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:55,641
That k is the cutoff to evaluate at, because
rank 30 versus rank 40 never reaches

28
00:01:55,641 --> 00:01:56,375
the model.

29
00:01:57,383 --> 00:01:59,208
Here's the design.

30
00:01:59,508 --> 00:02:01,733
On the right, the online path.

31
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:05,283
On the left, the measurement
loop that justifies it.

32
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:11,608
Online, the query goes to a hybrid first
stage, which returns 100 candidates.

33
00:02:11,908 --> 00:02:16,308
The cross-encoder reranks them, and
the top k go to the LLM.

34
00:02:16,917 --> 00:02:20,217
The reranker sits on the
latency-critical path.

35
00:02:20,517 --> 00:02:22,292
So give it a timeout.

36
00:02:22,592 --> 00:02:28,383
If it exceeds its budget, fall back to the
first-stage order, and the top k still go

37
00:02:28,383 --> 00:02:29,142
to the LLM.

38
00:02:29,442 --> 00:02:31,492
That bounds its worst case.

39
00:02:32,517 --> 00:02:34,967
Next, choose the candidate depth.

40
00:02:35,267 --> 00:02:36,942
Start at 100.

41
00:02:37,242 --> 00:02:42,292
Plot first-stage recall at N against N, and pick
the point where the curve flattens.

42
00:02:42,917 --> 00:02:45,917
That's often between 50 and 200.

43
00:02:46,217 --> 00:02:51,590
Reranking cost grows linearly with N, so
depth past the knee buys latency, and

44
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:52,417
little else.

45
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:55,317
Then choose the model.

46
00:02:55,617 --> 00:03:02,007
The default is a cross-encoder: a BERT,
MiniLM or XLM-R class model of roughly

47
00:03:02,007 --> 00:03:08,397
20-600M parameters, which reads each query
and passage pair jointly, with full

48
00:03:08,397 --> 00:03:09,217
attention.

49
00:03:09,817 --> 00:03:17,392
LLM rerankers, like RankGPT, are strong
zero-shot, but 10-100× more expensive.

50
00:03:17,692 --> 00:03:23,542
Use them to label data and distill a smaller
model, or for low-QPS, high-value

51
00:03:23,542 --> 00:03:24,142
queries.

52
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:30,192
And late interaction, like ColBERT, is a
cheaper middle ground, especially if you

53
00:03:30,192 --> 00:03:31,200
already run it.

54
00:03:32,217 --> 00:03:33,992
How fast is it?

55
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:41,842
Reranking 100 passages of about 256
tokens is 25,600 tokens.

56
00:03:42,142 --> 00:03:49,674
A 100M-parameter cross-encoder needs about 2
times 100M FLOPs per token, so about 5

57
00:03:49,674 --> 00:03:51,217
TFLOPs per query.

58
00:03:51,817 --> 00:04:00,907
A data-center GPU sustaining 50-150 TFLOPs in
fp16 finishes that in roughly 30-100

59
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:02,792
ms, when batched.

60
00:04:03,092 --> 00:04:08,242
A 22M-parameter MiniLM is about 5× faster.

61
00:04:08,542 --> 00:04:13,467
A 560M-parameter model is about 5× slower.

62
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:17,383
Benchmark on your own hardware
before you set N.

63
00:04:18,383 --> 00:04:23,233
Format the input with the title, the section
header and the chunk text together.

64
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,858
Headers carry a lot of signal.

65
00:04:26,483 --> 00:04:29,583
And check the model's
maximum input length.

66
00:04:29,883 --> 00:04:35,803
Many classic BERT and MiniLM-based
cross-encoders cap at 512 tokens, and anything

67
00:04:35,803 --> 00:04:41,283
longer is silently truncated, which can drop
the evidence from long chunks.

68
00:04:41,883 --> 00:04:44,933
For the output, take the top k by score.

69
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:51,100
Optionally, drop candidates below a calibrated
score threshold, so the LLM gets

70
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:52,808
fewer, better passages.

71
00:04:53,108 --> 00:04:58,583
That can cut context tokens substantially, which
can pay for the reranker by itself.

72
00:04:59,183 --> 00:05:04,862
As a second phase, fine-tune on in-domain pairs,
with hard negatives mined from the

73
00:05:04,862 --> 00:05:05,683
first stage.

74
00:05:05,983 --> 00:05:09,208
That commonly adds several nDCG points.

75
00:05:09,508 --> 00:05:14,653
And it's cheaper than fine-tuning the embedder,
because changing the reranker needs

76
00:05:14,653 --> 00:05:15,583
no re-indexing.

77
00:05:16,583 --> 00:05:18,183
One warning.

78
00:05:18,483 --> 00:05:21,758
Domain mismatch can make a
reranker harmful.

79
00:05:22,058 --> 00:05:27,853
In the LegalBench-RAG benchmark, a
general-purpose reranker performed worse than no

80
00:05:27,853 --> 00:05:29,458
reranker on legal text.

81
00:05:29,758 --> 00:05:31,633
That's why you measure.

82
00:05:32,650 --> 00:05:34,350
So, measuring it.

83
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:40,814
Start with 500-2,000 real queries,
stratified by type, with graded relevance

84
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:41,625
judgments.

85
00:05:42,250 --> 00:05:47,275
Pool the judgments from the top 20 of both
the baseline and the reranked list.

86
00:05:47,575 --> 00:05:53,050
Otherwise, relevant passages that only the
reranker surfaces count as misses.

87
00:05:53,650 --> 00:06:01,125
Measure at the cutoff the LLM sees: nDCG at
10, recall at 5 and at 10, and MRR.

88
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:06,350
And report first-stage recall at N
alongside, to show the ceiling.

89
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:11,025
Then compare per query: same
queries, two systems.

90
00:06:11,325 --> 00:06:17,400
Use a paired bootstrap or permutation test,
with 95% confidence intervals.

91
00:06:18,017 --> 00:06:20,242
How big a gain can you detect?

92
00:06:20,542 --> 00:06:27,892
Say the per-query difference in nDCG at 10 has
a standard deviation of about 0.25.

93
00:06:28,192 --> 00:06:36,215
On 1,000 queries, the standard error is about
0.008, so the 95% interval is about

94
00:06:36,215 --> 00:06:38,692
plus or minus 1.6 points.

95
00:06:39,317 --> 00:06:42,417
A gain of 2-3 points is detectable.

96
00:06:42,717 --> 00:06:44,867
A gain of 1 point is not.

97
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:48,667
And report wins, ties and
losses per query.

98
00:06:48,967 --> 00:06:54,917
A reranker that helps 30% of queries and
hurts 10% needs a look at the losers.

99
00:06:55,917 --> 00:07:01,796
Next, run the full RAG pipeline with and without
the reranker, holding the LLM, the

100
00:07:01,796 --> 00:07:03,142
prompt and k fixed.

101
00:07:03,442 --> 00:07:09,448
Measure answer correctness, faithfulness and
citation precision, with an LLM judge

102
00:07:09,448 --> 00:07:11,792
calibrated against human labels.

103
00:07:12,417 --> 00:07:16,867
Also run a variant with a smaller
k, say 5 instead of 10.

104
00:07:17,167 --> 00:07:21,342
A common win is the same answer
quality at half the tokens.

105
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:26,150
Slice by query type, length,
language and document type.

106
00:07:26,450 --> 00:07:32,316
Watch exact-match and ID queries in
particular: cross-encoders sometimes demote

107
00:07:32,316 --> 00:07:33,875
lexically exact hits.

108
00:07:34,483 --> 00:07:39,789
And ablate, to confirm the gain isn't just from
reranking deeper, or from a change

109
00:07:39,789 --> 00:07:41,083
in input formatting.

110
00:07:42,083 --> 00:07:47,701
Online, run an A/B test with a
pre-registered primary metric, such as answer

111
00:07:47,701 --> 00:07:48,883
acceptance rate.

112
00:07:49,483 --> 00:07:56,453
Set guardrails: p95 and p99 latency, timeout
rate, cost per query, and abstention

113
00:07:56,453 --> 00:07:56,883
rate.

114
00:07:57,483 --> 00:07:59,833
Do a power analysis up front.

115
00:08:00,133 --> 00:08:05,881
Small changes in noisy implicit-feedback metrics
need large samples, so compute the

116
00:08:05,881 --> 00:08:08,858
minimum detectable effect
before launching.

117
00:08:09,483 --> 00:08:13,108
For comparing ranking quality,
consider interleaving.

118
00:08:13,408 --> 00:08:18,900
It mixes both rankings into one result list per
user, and it's far more sensitive

119
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,408
per query than an A/B test on clicks.

120
00:08:21,708 --> 00:08:25,483
And monitor the reranker's score
distribution for drift.

121
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:28,258
Finally, cost.

122
00:08:28,558 --> 00:08:34,333
Present it as net cost: the reranker's cost
minus the generation tokens it saves.

123
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:43,756
One GPU at roughly $1 to $2 an hour, at 20-50
QPS, costs about 0.6 to 3 cents per

124
00:08:43,756 --> 00:08:45,333
1,000 queries.

125
00:08:45,950 --> 00:08:47,650
Now the savings.

126
00:08:47,950 --> 00:08:53,974
If the reranker lets you drop k from 20 to 8
chunks of about 500 tokens, that's

127
00:08:53,974 --> 00:08:57,100
about 6,000 fewer input tokens per query.

128
00:08:57,717 --> 00:09:03,583
At an illustrative $1 per million input
tokens, that saves about $6 per 1,000

129
00:09:03,583 --> 00:09:08,992
queries, two to three orders of magnitude
more than the reranker costs.

130
00:09:09,292 --> 00:09:13,617
So when it lets you shrink k, the
reranker usually pays for itself.

131
00:09:14,617 --> 00:09:16,442
Here are the options.

132
00:09:16,742 --> 00:09:21,517
No reranker has the lowest latency, but
lower precision at small k.

133
00:09:22,117 --> 00:09:30,139
A small cross-encoder, around 20-100M
parameters, takes 20-60 ms for a big precision

134
00:09:30,139 --> 00:09:30,617
gain.

135
00:09:30,917 --> 00:09:33,317
That's the default production choice.

136
00:09:33,917 --> 00:09:41,512
A large cross-encoder or a hosted API is higher
quality and multilingual, at 100-300

137
00:09:41,512 --> 00:09:43,592
ms and a per-call cost.

138
00:09:43,892 --> 00:09:50,798
And LLM listwise reranking costs 10-100× more,
with seconds of latency, so keep it

139
00:09:50,798 --> 00:09:52,567
for offline labeling.

140
00:09:52,867 --> 00:09:59,276
Remember too that offline nDCG doesn't always
translate end to end, so measure both

141
00:09:59,276 --> 00:09:59,817
levels.

142
00:10:00,817 --> 00:10:02,717
Now the common mistakes.

143
00:10:03,017 --> 00:10:09,535
Reporting only the mean nDCG gain, with no
paired test, no slices, and no per-query

144
00:10:09,535 --> 00:10:10,792
wins and losses.

145
00:10:11,092 --> 00:10:14,167
Noise and slice regressions ship as wins.

146
00:10:14,783 --> 00:10:17,108
Ignoring first-stage recall.

147
00:10:17,408 --> 00:10:21,058
A reranker can't recover
documents it never sees.

148
00:10:21,683 --> 00:10:23,408
Pooling bias.

149
00:10:23,708 --> 00:10:28,358
Judging only the baseline's results
understates the reranker's gains.

150
00:10:28,658 --> 00:10:32,183
Pooling only the reranker's
results overstates them.

151
00:10:32,783 --> 00:10:34,708
Silent truncation.

152
00:10:35,008 --> 00:10:39,133
Long chunks cut at the input limit
drop the relevant span.

153
00:10:39,750 --> 00:10:42,025
And forgetting the latency tail.

154
00:10:42,325 --> 00:10:48,260
Without a timeout fallback, and known p99
behavior under load, the reranker becomes

155
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:49,475
an outage source.

156
00:10:50,483 --> 00:10:53,133
Which leads to the most useful habit.

157
00:10:53,433 --> 00:10:56,208
Write the ship rule before the experiment.

158
00:10:56,508 --> 00:11:01,492
For example: ship if end-to-end correctness
gains at least 2 points, with a

159
00:11:01,492 --> 00:11:04,083
confidence interval that excludes zero.

160
00:11:04,683 --> 00:11:08,433
No important slice regresses by
more than 1 point.

161
00:11:08,733 --> 00:11:14,408
And p95 latency rises by no
more than 150 ms.

162
00:11:14,708 --> 00:11:19,033
Pre-registering the rule prevents
rationalizing after the fact.

163
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:21,725
So, to recap.

164
00:11:22,025 --> 00:11:25,500
Check first-stage recall before
you add a reranker.

165
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:31,550
Measure it at the cutoff the LLM sees, per
query, with a paired test and slices.

166
00:11:31,850 --> 00:11:33,825
Give it a timeout fallback.

167
00:11:34,125 --> 00:11:35,975
Count its net cost.

168
00:11:36,275 --> 00:11:39,675
And write the ship rule before
you run the experiment.

169
00:11:40,683 --> 00:11:44,208
The full article, with every
source, is on vidyal.ai.

170
00:11:45,217 --> 00:11:46,992
Thanks for watching.

171
00:11:47,292 --> 00:11:53,117
Next in the series, cutting LLM cost by
70% without losing quality.

172
00:11:53,417 --> 00:11:55,542
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
